/* book Worked example: Q: A first-order low-pass filter has R = 1 kΩ and C = 0.1 µF. Find its cutoff frequency fc and its gain at 10 kHz in dB. A differential amplifier has Ad = 1000 and Acm = 0.5. Find its CMRR.
   1. Convert R = 1 kΩ = 1000 Ω and C = 0.1 µF = 0.1 × 10⁻⁶ F = 1 × 10⁻⁷ F.
   2. Use fc = 1/(2πRC). fc = 1/(2π × 1000 × 1 × 10⁻⁷) = 1/(6.283 × 10⁻⁴) = 1591.55 Hz.
   3. At f = 10 kHz = 10,000 Hz, the ratio is f/fc = 10,000/1591.55 = 6.283.
   4. The low-pass gain is |H| = 1/√(1 + (f/fc)²) = 1/√(1 + 6.283²) = 1/√(1 + 39.48) = 1/√40.48 = 1/6.362 = 0.1572.
   5. In decibels, gain = 20 log₁₀(0.1572) = 20 × (−0.8036) = −16.07 dB.
   6. For the differential amplifier, Ad/Acm = 1000/0.5 = 2000.
   7. CMRR = 20 log₁₀(2000) = 20 × 3.3010 = 66.02 dB.
   Answer: fc = 1591.55 Hz (about 1592 Hz), gain at 10 kHz = 0.1572 (−16.07 dB), and CMRR = 66.02 dB (about 66 dB). */
