/* book Worked example 2: Q: An ECG amplifier has a differential gain of 1000. The heart gives 1 mV and the body carries 0.5 V of 50 Hz common-mode voltage. (a) Find the output signal and the output hum for CMRR = 100 dB and for CMRR = 60 dB. (b) The offset is removed with a first-order high-pass of R = 3.3 MΩ and C = 1 µF; find its cutoff.
   1. (a) Output signal = Ad × 1 mV = 1000 × 1 mV = 1.0 V.
   2. At 100 dB: Ad/Acm = 10⁵ (since 20 log₁₀ 10⁵ = 100 dB), so Acm = 1000 / 10⁵ = 0.01 and the hum = 0.01 × 0.5 V = 5 mV, 200 times smaller than the signal.
   3. At 60 dB: Ad/Acm = 10³ (20 log₁₀ 10³ = 60 dB), so Acm = 1 and the hum = 0.5 V, half the size of the heartbeat; the trace would be unreadable.
   4. (b) fc = 1/(2πRC) = 1/(2π × 3.3 × 10⁶ × 1 × 10⁻⁶) = 1/20.73 = 0.048 Hz.
   5. Check: 0.048 Hz is below the 0.05 Hz diagnostic limit, so the slow ST segment of the ECG passes while the electrode offset is blocked.
   Answer: The signal is 1.0 V; the hum is 5 mV at 100 dB CMRR but 0.5 V at 60 dB; the high-pass cuts off at 0.048 Hz. */
