/* book: the chapter, paragraph by paragraph (first sentences):
   - A digital voltmeter (DVM) converts an input voltage into a display number.
   - range is the full-scale voltage of the selected range, in V; counts is the number of steps the display can show; resolution is the smallest voltage change the display can show, in V.
   - A 3½ digit display has three full digits and one half digit that is only 0 or 1.
   - Accuracy is quoted against reading, not range.
   - A multimeter adds current, resistance and continuity.
   - The cathode ray tube (CRT) is a vacuum glass envelope.
   - D is spot deflection on the screen, in m; L is distance from plate centre to screen; l is plate length; d is plate separation; Vd is voltage between the plates; Va is accelerating voltage; S is deflection sensitivity, in m/V.
   - The panel quotes volts per division.
   - A trigger starts the ramp only when the input crosses a chosen level in a chosen direction.
   - T is one sweep time in s; time/div is the panel setting in s/div; divisions is the number of horizontal divisions the sweep covers (usually 10); f is the frequency of the displayed waveform in Hz.
*/
